  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule are both in place, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished cleanly with no alerts firing.\n\n**What the data holds:** there are 14,074 web requests in `kibana_sample_data_logs`, timestamped from 4 October to 3 December 2026. Fields include the client IP, URL, host, response code, bytes sent, OS, user agent, file extension, and source and destination country. Response codes break down as 12,832 \u00d7 200, 801 \u00d7 404 and 441 \u00d7 503, so 503 is the only 5xx code present. One oddity: every request has `geo.src` = \"US\", so I used `geo.dest` for the country breakdown instead.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-42b024e4 Web logs overview\"** (id `9b305fd6-435e-4bd3-a502-d4dfa5e48037`, default time range is the last 7 days). It has seven panels:\n- Four headline numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs), average response size in bytes, and the count of 5xx responses.\n- A stacked bar chart of requests over time, split by response code.\n- A chart of the 10 most-requested URLs.\n- A chart of the top 10 countries by `geo.dest`.\n\nI checked that the queries behind these panels return data, but I haven't looked at the dashboard in the browser.\n\n**Rule: \"EFF-42b024e4 5xx spike\"** (id `2d39d1ef-5bf4-408b-805d-e4ebc86aa61c`):\n- It runs every minute and counts requests on the `timestamp` field with a response code from 500 to 599.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10 within 5 minutes.\n- I set it to count every matching request in the 5-minute window each time it runs, including ones already counted the minute before. Otherwise it would only count new requests and undercount the window.\n- **No notification is attached.** When it fires, the alert only shows up in Kibana; nothing is sent anywhere. Adding an action (email, Slack, etc.) would need a connector.",
